  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rainfall continues today across western NY with the \nremnants of post tropical cyclone Isaias.\n\nLatest satellite imagery shows a large scale upper low across \neastern KY drifting east with the remnants of Isaias. Ahead of the \nlow, deep southerly moisture transport has led to extensive cloud \ncover and rain for much of the eastern seaboard. Heaviest \nprecipitation early this afternoon has been placed across central \nand eastern PA while much of western NY remains just outside the \narea of best synoptic scale forcing. Widespread, stronger returns as \nnoted by regional radars will begin shifting across the NY/PA border \nthis afternoon. NBM probability of exceeding 0.25\" of rainfall \nmaximizes from the Southern Tier into the Finger Lakes region \nthrough the remainder of the Sunday. Rainfall will taper off from \nwest to east overnight into early Monday morning as the upper level \ndisturbance shifts east across the Mid-Atlantic and a ridge of high \npressure builds up from the south.\n\nFor far western NY, including the Niagara Frontier, minimal rainfall \nhas been reported as of midday Sunday. This was being hinted at with \nthe eastward shifts in model guidance over the past 24-36 hours for \nthe main precipitation shield. However, a deformation band \ndeveloping over southwestern Ontario will begin to shift eastward \nlater this afternoon and provide another chance precipitation with \nthis system, though accumulations will likely max out near a tenth \nof an inch.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The next round of rain showers arrives late Monday \nnight with strong thunderstorms possible across western NY Tuesday.\n\nThe next low pressure system will pass east across the Great Lakes \nlate Monday and into Tuesday, pushing a cold front southeast across \nthe region Tuesday evening. Ahead of the front and aligned with a \nleading shortwave, expect another round of rain showers Tuesday from \nthe Niagara Frontier east towards the North Country. A few \nthunderstorms can't be ruled out as mid-level lapse rates approach 7 \nC/km with the presence of an EML and effective shear of 35-40 kt \nthanks to a 50 kt jet at 700mb. \n\nWhile there still remains quite a bit of uncertainty with high \ntemperatures across the region Tuesday, latest ensemble trends have \nshifted the track of the low slightly farther north. This will allow \nportions of western NY to remain under better warm southwesterly \nflow as 850mb temps climb around 14-16 degC, near the climatological \n99th percentile for mid-October. This could support temperatures 10 \ndegF above normal, especially across the Southern Tier. NBM shows \nTuesday afternoon highs for locations north of I-90 with \nconsiderable spread still mainly tied to the timing of \nprecipitation, cloud cover, and the frontal passage. \n\nThis being said, storms will likely remain elevated due to poor low-\nlevel lapse rates, but additional shifts in the low track could \nprovide more limited cloud cover improving boundary layer \ndestabilization and potential for afternoon thunderstorm development \nover western NY. SPC has highlighted western NY with a Day 3 \nMarginal Risk for severe storms.",
